WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.950
My first security job was at a

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.130
mobile money company.

3
00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.830
Every morning, our team asked one

4
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.230
question: what tried to get in last night?

5
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.620
I haven’t been bored since.

6
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.120
Today: careers in cyber security — the

7
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.000
jobs, the rules, and your first steps.

8
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.280
Every bank, phone company, hospital,

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.260
government and school needs defenders.

10
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.640
Around the world, millions of security

11
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.720
jobs are waiting for people to fill them.

12
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.970
And teams need people of every background:

13
00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.830
women and men, from every country.

14
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.310
Here are some of the jobs.

15
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.000
Security analysts watch the alarms and

16
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.540
check the messages people report.

17
00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.870
Security engineers build defences,

18
00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.950
like the sign-in systems you use.

19
00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.050
And ethical hackers test systems to

20
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.150
find weak spots before criminals do,

21
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.070
but only with written permission.

22
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:01.890
There’s more.

23
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.840
Forensic investigators work out what

24
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.800
happened after an attack, and incident

25
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.400
responders lead the clean-up.

26
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.330
Cryptographers design the maths

27
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.850
behind locks and keys.

28
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.800
Privacy officers make sure people’s

29
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.760
data is treated fairly, and trainers

30
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.760
teach everyone to spot tricks.

31
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.190
One rule matters above all.

32
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.360
Getting into a system without

33
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.660
permission is a crime in almost

34
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.440
every country, even “just to look”.

35
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.100
Real defenders get written permission

36
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.400
first, and stop exactly where it ends.

37
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:40.150
Quick check!

38
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.450
A friend finds the office computer’s

39
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.710
password on a sticky note and wants

40
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.730
to “just look” around it.

41
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.060
What do you do?

42
00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.850
Say no, and tell the IT team

43
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:53.770
where the password was.

44
00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:55.930
Looking without permission

45
00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.590
already breaks the law.

46
00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.370
Telling someone who can fix it is

47
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.210
exactly what a defender does.

48
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.920
You don’t need to be a genius.

49
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.250
You need curiosity, patience

50
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.410
with puzzles, and honesty.

51
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.560
Maths, computing and languages all help,

52
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.260
and so does explaining things clearly,

53
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.960
because defenders often explain problems

54
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.560
to people who aren’t experts.

55
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:23.800
You can start now.

56
00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.550
Join or start a computing club.

57
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.050
Try capture-the-flag competitions

58
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.190
made for students, where you solve

59
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.650
security puzzles legally.

60
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.340
And take free online courses, practising

61
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.300
only on labs built for practice.

62
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:40.150
Your turn.

63
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.540
While checking your own results, you see

64
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.700
another student’s details on the

65
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.980
university’s student portal by mistake.

66
00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.810
What’s the ethical next step?

67
00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.050
Tell the university’s IT team

68
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.990
privately, and go no further: don’t

69
00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.710
test more, and don’t share it.

70
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.370
Remember: defenders are needed

71
00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.670
everywhere, there are many kinds of

72
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.270
jobs, and permission comes first, always.

73
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.540
Try it tomorrow: in the lab, train a

74
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.580
phishing filter with examples, then test

75
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.600
it with a tricky message it gets wrong.

76
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.370
Then pick one security job, find out what

77
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.590
a day in it looks like, and tell a friend.
